// Arm64Runtime — the arm64 OS boundary: HOW this backend reaches the kernel, and the entry stub
// `mrt_start` that bridges Maxon's `main` to process exit. The arm64 twin of `X64Runtime`.
//
// ⭐ ONE FACT SPLITS THE TWO arm64 OSes, AND IT IS NOT THE OBJECT FORMAT. **macOS forbids raw
// syscalls**: every OS primitive must go through libSystem, which forces a dynamic linker, a GOT,
// dyld chained fixups, ad-hoc code signing and an LC_UUID. **Linux permits them**, so the same
// primitives become a bare `svc #0` and NONE of that apparatus exists — no PT_INTERP, no GOT, no
// import table, a static ET_EXEC. That single difference is why the Mach-O writer is 948 lines and
// the ELF writer is a third of it, and it is asked here, once, by `arm64OsCallKind`.
//
// ⭐ THE STUB IS HAND-ASSEMBLED, NOT A MODULE FUNCTION. x64's `mrt_start` is built from `TargetOp`s
// and prepended to the module (`prependEntryStub`), because every instruction it needs — `push`,
// `call rel32`, the IAT `call [rip]` — is a `TargetOp`. The arm64 stub cannot be: on macOS it
// terminates through a dyld-bound GOT slot, which has no `TargetOp` channel outside this backend.
// So it is hand-assembled here into a `FunctionCodeChunk` from `Arm64Backend`'s encoder primitives,
// exactly as `X64Runtime` hand-assembles its panic runtime. The chunk is laid out FIRST in `.text`
// (`buildArm64Backend`), so the image's entry point is its offset.
//
// THE SCALAR CORE NEEDS NO RUNTIME FLOOR. It allocates nothing (no slab/MM init, no leak check), it
// spawns nothing (no scheduler), and its only trap-shaped case — divide-by-zero — is defined to
// return 0 on AArch64 (spec ruling; see `StdToArm64Conversion`), so there is no fault handler and no
// `mrt_runtime_init` to call before user code. The whole runtime is therefore this one stub; the MM
// and green-thread floors GROW back at their milestones exactly as x64's do.

// How the arm64 backend reaches the OS. ONE door, asked by every OS-primitive lowering
// (`osExit`/`osWriteStdout`/`osAllocPages`) and by the entry stub below, so those four sites cannot
// drift on which OSes this backend supports or on how each one gets there.
export enum Arm64OsCallKind
	// A GOT-indirect libSystem call (`adrp x16, GOTPAGE; ldr x16, [x16, GOTOFF]; blr x16`), the slot
	// named by an `OsImportSlot` and bound by dyld. macOS's only route.
	importCall
	// A raw supervisor call (`movz x8, #number; svc #0`), the number named per-primitive. Linux's.
	syscall
end 'Arm64OsCallKind'

// Exported: the isel (`StdToArm64Conversion`) asks it for every OS-primitive lowering and for the
// mmap flag word, and this stub asks it for its exit route — the one place either can ask.
export function arm64OsCallKind(os Os) returns Arm64OsCallKind
	return match os 'os'
		macos gives Arm64OsCallKind.importCall
		linux gives Arm64OsCallKind.syscall
		windows panic("arm64OsCallKind: there is no arm64-windows backend — `buildBackend` dispatches arm64 to this backend only for macos/linux")
		wasi panic("arm64OsCallKind: wasm32-wasi is not an arm64 target — the wasm backend reaches the OS through WASI imports, not through this file")
	end 'os'
end 'arm64OsCallKind'

// --- Linux syscall numbers (AArch64) ---
//
// The AArch64 table, NOT x86-64's: `write` is 64 here and 1 there, `mmap` 222 and 9. Named because
// a bare 64 at a call site is unreadable and the number IS the primitive's identity.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE `Arm64` PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT DECORATION — IT WAS THE BUG** (found by A1y's review).
// These three were spelled `LinuxSyscallWrite`/`LinuxSyscallMmap`/`LinuxSyscallExitGroup`, and
// `Targets/X64/X64LinuxRuntime` exported THE SAME THREE NAMES over the x86-64 numbers. Maxon's top-level
// name space is FLAT: a declaration's directory grants it nothing, `export` publishes to the whole
// program, and two exported globals of one name are resolved SILENTLY BY FILE ORDER with no diagnostic
// (probed against the bootstrap — the reader's own directory is irrelevant, and the winner follows
// POSITION, not value). x64's file came last, so every CROSS-FILE read lost, including this backend's.
//
// MEASURED on arm64-linux, by disassembling every `svc #0` in a cross-compiled binary:
// `StdToArm64Conversion.lowerOsExit`/`lowerPanic` emitted **231**, `lowerOsWrite` **1** and
// `lowerOsMmap` **9** — the x86-64 numbers. On AArch64 those are `munlockall`, `io_destroy` and
// `lgetxattr`: none exits, writes or maps, and none fails loudly, so a violated range guard did not
// abort at all — it fell through to `mov x0, xzr; ret` and the program carried on with a zero. The ONE
// use that was right is the entry stub's below, because a file's own declaration shadows the import.
//
// ⭐ The committed `specs-shv2/fragments/arm64-linux/**` goldens record `arm64.syscall 94` at all 97
// sites, so this restores agreement with them rather than moving them — and their disagreement with what
// this compiler emitted was the tell. ⚠ THOSE GOLDENS WERE PRODUCED ON THE arm64 HOST, AFTER the
// collision existed, which means the losing declaration was not even the SAME one on both machines: file
// order is a property of the CHECKOUT, so a name collision makes the emitted code depend on which box
// compiled the compiler. That is the strongest reason the cure is a name and not a comment, and it is why
// this must never be "left for the lane that can run it" — nothing about it is arm64-specific but the
// numbers. The comment two paragraphs up said "NOT x86-64's" the whole time,
// which is the project's cure ladder (DERIVE > CHECK > COMMENT) failing at its bottom rung: a comment
// cannot make a name resolve. One name per fact can.
//
// ⚠ `exit_group` (94), NOT `exit` (93) — a DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE from v1, which emits 93. `exit`
// terminates the calling THREAD; `exit_group` terminates the whole thread group, which is what a C
// `_exit` actually invokes and what `main` returning means. The two are indistinguishable in a
// single-threaded process, so this costs nothing today and stays correct the day shv2 grows OS
// threads (P1.5) — at which point 93 would silently leave the other threads running.
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallWrite = 64
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallMmap = 222
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallExitGroup = 94

// F1a — the page-LIFECYCLE half of the mapping ABI: `munmap` releases a mapping, `madvise` drops its physical
// backing while keeping the mapping. AArch64's numbers, NOT x86-64's (11 and 28 there — see the twin block in
// `Targets/X64/X64LinuxRuntime.maxon`), which is the whole reason this table carries an arch prefix. There is
// deliberately no recommit number: a `MADV_DONTNEED`ed page refaults on the next touch, so re-backing one is a
// Windows-only call and arm64 emits nothing for it.
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallMunmap = 215
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallMadvise = 233

// F2 — the per-thread identity word. `gettid` answers the calling thread's kernel thread id: per-thread
// stable and never 0, which is exactly `StdOp.currentThreadToken`'s contract. It is asked instead of reading
// `TPIDR_EL0` because shv2's Linux lane is a raw static binary with no libc, so nothing has written that
// register and it reads 0 — the op's own header carries the full argument. 178 on AArch64, 186 on x86-64
// (`X64LinuxRuntime`'s twin), which is exactly why these numbers wear the arch prefix.
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallGettid = 178

// Build `mrt_start` and return it as a `FunctionCodeChunk`. It bridges Maxon's `main` (AAPCS64,
// ExitCode in x0) to the platform's process-exit primitive — which takes its code in x0 too, so the
// value flows through with no move on either OS:
//
//   macOS:                                   Linux:
//     [bl __module_init]                       [bl __module_init]
//     bl   main                                bl   main
//     [bl __maxon_global_cleanup]              [bl __maxon_global_cleanup]
//     adrp x16, _exit@GOTPAGE                  movz x8, #94        ; __NR_exit_group
//     ldr  x16, [x16, _exit@GOTOFF]            svc  #0
//     blr  x16
//     brk  #0                                  brk  #0
//
// NO FRAME, and the reason differs per OS but the conclusion does not. The stub never touches sp, so
// the OS's 16-byte-aligned entry sp survives to the `bl` by construction. On macOS dyld enters through
// a call-like path; on Linux the kernel enters `_start` with **x30 UNDEFINED** — there is no return
// address — so the stub must never `ret`. It does not: both routes end in a non-returning exit, and
// the `brk #0` catches the impossible fall-through rather than letting execution run off into the next
// function.
//
// ⚠ **THE SECOND HALF OF THAT ARGUMENT USED TO BE "the scalar core has no backtrace that would need
// x29/x30 chained", AND SINCE A3n THERE IS ONE** (`Arm64PanicRuntime.buildArm64WalkFramesChunk`). The
// stub stays frameless anyway, but for a DIFFERENT reason, and the difference matters to anyone editing
// either side: `mrt_start` is never a LINK in the chain, only its last symbolized NAME. `main`'s own
// frame record carries the return address INTO this stub at `[x29+8]`, which is what prints `in
// mrt_start`; the walk then follows `[x29+0]`, which is whatever the loader left in x29 because this
// stub never writes it. Two INDEPENDENT things stop it there, and neither is the chain: the walk's
// TEXT GUARD rejects any return address outside `[mrt_start, __symtable)` (measured on macOS — dyld's
// x29 is a live stack address, the load succeeds, and the address above it is not this image's code),
// and a `cbz` catches a null frame pointer (Linux, where execve zeroes the GPRs). Give this stub a
// frame record, or drop either guard, and the walk's termination changes.
//
// ⭐⭐ **argv IS CAPTURED HERE, AND WHERE IT IS CAPTURED FROM IS PER-OS WITHIN arm64 (MAC7).** This
// paragraph was a landmine note and is now the implementation of what it warned about. dyld hands
// macOS `mrt_start` argc/argv in **x0/x1**. The Linux kernel does NOT: it enters with **argc at
// `[sp]`, argv at `sp+8`, envp after its NUL terminator**, and x0/x1 architecturally UNSPECIFIED.
// v1 stashes from x0/x1 unconditionally on both (`Arm64Dialect.maxon:266-272`,
// `Arm64PrologueEpilogue.maxon:42-64`), which is a latent bug on Linux and is the reason this arm
// is a `match os` rather than one sequence.
//
// ⚠ **THE CAPTURE IS THE FIRST THING THE STUB DOES, BEFORE ANY `bl`.** On macOS a call would clobber
// x0/x1; on Linux — where the capture must read the stack — anything that perturbs sp would move the
// two words out from under it. Both hazards are avoided by the same placement, which is why it is
// stated once rather than per arm.
//
// `leakCheck` gates the future MM leak bridge exactly as the x64 stub does; it is `false` for the
// scalar core, which has no allocator and cannot leak. `initGlobals` gates the managed-global
// initialization and release the same way (P1.7 slice 2a) — `false` for a program with no managed
// top-level `var`, which is every program in the scalar corpus.
export function buildArm64EntryStubChunk(leakCheck bool, initGlobals bool, drainGreenThreads bool, captureArgv bool, os Os) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	var callFixups = CallFixupArray.create()
	var iatRelocs = RelocationArray.create()
	var rdataRelocs = RdataRelocationArray.create()

	// The argument vector into the two `.data` words `__cl_count`/`__cl_arg` read (MAC7). It rides
	// `usesCommandLine`, so a program that never asks for its arguments carries neither the capture nor
	// the words — the one-bit-two-tiers arrangement every other runtime family here uses.
	if captureArgv 'argv'
		emitArm64ArgvCapture(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, os: os)
	end 'argv'

	// Fill every EAGER managed global's `.data` slot BEFORE user code (P1.7 slice 2a) — a `static` member's
	// is filled on first access instead (see `ModuleInit`). `__module_init` takes
	// no argument and returns nothing, so there is nothing to move around it.
	if initGlobals 'moduleInit'
		arm64EmitBranchLink(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: ModuleInitName)
	end 'moduleInit'

	// bl main — filed as a CallFixup; concat resolves it to main's `.text` offset (mirrors x64's
	// `callDirect(UserEntryName)`). `UserEntryName` is the canonical `String` spelling; the fixup
	// matches against the function's `ByteArray` name, so it is rendered to bytes here.
	arm64EmitBranchLink(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: UserEntryName.toByteArray())

	// Release every managed global AFTER `main` and BEFORE the leak gate — a global released after the
	// gate would count as a leak (exit 101). `__maxon_global_cleanup` takes the ExitCode in x0 and hands
	// it back in x0, the same pass-through shape `__mm_leak_check` has, so on AAPCS64 the value flows
	// through both with no move at all.
	if initGlobals 'globalCleanup'
		arm64EmitBranchLink(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GlobalCleanupName)
	end 'globalCleanup'

	// ⭐⭐ **RUN THE SCHEDULER OUT AND JOIN ITS WORKER Ms, AFTER `main` AND BEFORE THE LEAK GATE (MAC3).**
	// The ORDER is the whole point and it is `BackendDispatch.appendGreenThreadDrainGate`'s argument, which
	// this stub had no arm for until the scheduler reached this lane: AFTER the global cleanup, because a
	// released managed global may be the last owner of a handle whose drop lets a green thread finish; and
	// BEFORE `__mm_leak_check`, because a worker M's `__gt_reclaim` steps the very counters that gate reads,
	// so an un-joined scheduler makes a clean program report 101 intermittently.
	//
	// `__gt_drain_at_exit` is shaped like `__mm_leak_check` deliberately — ExitCode in x0, ExitCode out — so
	// on AAPCS64 the value flows through it with no move at all, exactly as it does through the two gates
	// around it. A program with no scheduler carries neither the call nor the function.
	if drainGreenThreads 'greenThreadDrain'
		arm64EmitBranchLink(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: GtDrainAtExitName)
	end 'greenThreadDrain'

	// The leak gate (future MM milestone). `__mm_leak_check` is an ordinary AAPCS64 function — it
	// takes the ExitCode in x0 and returns the adjusted code (or 101) in x0 — so the value flows
	// straight through with no move. Never emitted for the scalar core (`leakCheck` is false).
	if leakCheck 'leakGate'
		arm64EmitBranchLink(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: MmLeakCheckName)
	end 'leakGate'

	// Terminate with the ExitCode already in x0. macOS records the `_exit` GOT slot as a Relocation
	// the Mach-O writer patches (the twin of x64's `iatCall` → ExitProcess); Linux needs no
	// relocation at all, which is the whole shape of this rung.
	match arm64OsCallKind(os) 'exitRoute'
		importCall then arm64EmitImportCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: osImportSlotIndex(OsImportSlot.exit))
		syscall then arm64EmitSyscall(code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallExitGroup)
	end 'exitRoute'

	// brk #0 — SIGTRAP if the exit primitive ever returns (it does not); stops execution running off
	// the end of this chunk into the next function rather than trusting it never to return.
	arm64EmitBrk(code)

	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(EntryPointName, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildArm64EntryStubChunk'

// ⭐ **STASH `argc` AND `argv` WHERE THE Std TIER CAN READ THEM.** Two `.data` words and two stores; the
// only per-OS part is which two places the pair is read FROM.
//
// ⚠ **THE ADDRESS GOES THROUGH x16 (IP0), WHICH IS FREE AT PROCESS ENTRY AND IS THE ONE SCRATCH NO ABI
// CONTRACT HERE CLAIMS.** x0/x1 hold the values being stored on macOS, and the mandated ADRP+ADD pair needs
// a destination that is neither.
function emitArm64ArgvCapture(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, os Os)
	match os 'argvLocation'
		macos then emitArm64ArgvFromEntryRegisters(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs)
		wasi then panic("buildArm64EntryStubChunk: `wasm32-wasi` is not an arm64 lane — a WASI component has no `mrt_start` and no arm64 `.text` at all, so an `Os.wasi` reaching this stub is a target-construction bug")
		linux then panic("buildArm64EntryStubChunk: arm64-Linux enters `_start` with argc at [sp] and argv at sp+8, and with x0/x1 architecturally UNSPECIFIED — so this capture cannot be the macOS one and `targetProvidesFacility` gives `commandLine` to arm64-macOS alone, which refuses such a program at its own source span (E3104). Reaching here means a program carrying `usesCommandLine` got past that gate on a Linux target, which is a compiler bug rather than the user-facing rejection")
		windows then panic("buildArm64EntryStubChunk: there is no arm64-Windows lane in this compiler — `Target` admits the pair only through `arm64OsCallKind`'s two OSes, so an `Os.windows` reaching the arm64 entry stub is a target-construction bug")
	end 'argvLocation'
end 'emitArm64ArgvCapture'

// dyld enters `mrt_start` with `argc` in x0 and `argv` in x1 — the two registers an AAPCS64 `main(argc,
// argv)` would have received, which is exactly how the platform's own start-up files read them.
function emitArm64ArgvFromEntryRegisters(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray)
	emitArm64ArgvStore(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, label: CommandLineArgcLabel, src: Arm64Register.x0)
	emitArm64ArgvStore(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, label: CommandLineArgvLabel, src: Arm64Register.x1)
end 'emitArm64ArgvFromEntryRegisters'

// One captured register into one `.data` word.
function emitArm64ArgvStore(code ByteArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, label ByteArray, src Arm64Register)
	arm64EmitAdrpAddPair(code, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x16), label: label, adrpKind: RdataRelocKind.dataSectionArm64Adrp, pageOffKind: RdataRelocKind.dataSectionArm64PageOff)
	arm64EmitScaledMem(code, opcode: Arm64OpStrX, rt: arm64RuntimeReg(src), baseReg: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x16), byteOffset: 0, scale: SpillSlotBytes)
end 'emitArm64ArgvStore'
